                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "probably THE single most underrated game in my steam library currently. don't know what volume that speaks to but all i can say is that DR2C is [b] sooo [/b] slept on. ngl it's genuinely kind of a crime that this game isn't considered an all-time great like so many other roguelikes of its era or genre. it's THAT good, i promise you\n\nthe game starts with a generally pretty steep learning curve (as most, if not every game of this genre does) with you having to learn what roadside events you run into--hell, there's some that i haven't found out about, even as of writing this review--learning about how the combat works and what to do in any given zombie-ridden death-infused scenario (trust me, you will die a metric FUCKton on the way there), but stick with it enough and the reward you get is one of the most in-depth, well developed and written, and, maybe a bit unflamboyant but deeply hilarious games i've ever played.\n\nthere's so much thought and effort and most importantly care put into simply every part of this game, between the aesthetic, the fighting mechanics (among other stats and hidden combos and a massive portfolio of character traits and perks that would just take me WAAAY too much time to properly sit through to fully explain), the soundtrack (my good LORD do the songs hit so god fucking damn well, it's nasty), and just about anything else you can honestly think of. plus, the devs still give death road an incredible amount of love and devotion that keeps giving the game updates, which is nothing short of admirable in this age of gaming.\n\ni might add that getting remote play set up is sometimes off-putting and can take moments where troubleshoots consume a bloated number of tries to configure everything right, but multiplayer in DR2C absolutely slaps so bangin' hard, it's nuts.\n\n9.8/10: death road to canada is such an awesome thing of batsh*t crazy, zombie-killing cheer that stuck to me like glue when i first opened it, and i doubt it will ever stop being as stupidly funny and fun to drive a seemingly disjointed ragtag group of morons, imbeciles, and--above anything else--complete [b] DINGUSES [/b] to the sparkly, snowy end of the road",
